# Changelog updates (user-facing)

**This rule is for user-visible changes only.** This feeds `web/lib/changelog.ts` (the "What's new" modal in the app).

For developer-facing technical notes (breaking changes, migrations, internal refactors), see `CHANGELOG.md` (the developer changelog) — that's updated separately.

Every change a user would notice (new feature, pricing/limit change, UI
change, a bug fix they'd feel) needs one entry in `web/lib/changelog.ts`
before the work is considered done. Purely internal changes (refactors,
tests, infra, docs) do not need an entry here.

## How to add an entry

1. Open `web/lib/changelog.ts`.
2. Add a new entry at the **top** of the `CHANGELOG` array (newest first)
   with today's date, a new `version` (use `YYYY.MM.DD`; append `-2`, `-3`,
   etc. if shipping more than once in a day), and 1-4 `items`.
3. If an entry for today's date/version already exists, append to its
   `items` instead of creating a new one.

## Writing the items

- Write for the end user, not for another engineer: say what changed for
  them, not how it was implemented.
- One short sentence per bullet. No jargon, no internal names (endpoints,
  table/column names, PR numbers, file paths).
- No em dashes. Ever. See `.cursor/rules/no-em-dashes.mdc`.
- Skip anything invisible to users (refactors, perf work with no visible
  effect, added tests, internal tooling).
- State the outcome, not the mechanics. Don't turn a bullet into pricing/
  quota documentation. No exact quotas, cap amounts, or "how the billing
  system decides this" explanations. That belongs on the pricing/settings
  page, not the changelog.

Good: "Pro now includes more chat each month at no extra charge."
Bad: "Pro now includes 68 Claude replies/mo; overage is $0.08/reply up to
your spend cap, tracked in Redis."

The "What's new" modal, header badge, and first-login popup all key off
`LATEST_VERSION`, which is derived automatically from the first entry.
Never hand-edit that constant.
